A||N||19||Monkeyman||Questions about the Air Force Reserves||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||07-31-2001||11:23 PM||||I'm considering enlisting in the Air Force Reserves and would like to know if anyone has any experience with them.  I'm prior military (Army and Indiana Army Nat'l Guard in the late '80s) so I know what the military as a whole is about but I don't know anything specific about the Air Force.  I'm 33 and don't want a new career (actually a new career would be nice, but not in the military).  Seems like the Reserves would be the way to go for some extra money and possibly some educational opportunities.<P>The Army is out as I've dealt with them before (and wasn't very impressed) and I have no intention of becoming a jarhead (nothing personal, Marines) or having to swim 3000 miles if my ship sinks.<P>What's basic training like in the Air Force?  What is AIT/Tech school like?  Anyone in the Intelligence field?  I was in communications in the Army and went nowhere with it.<P>Post here or email me at: <A HREF="mailto:dandc1@hoosierlink.com">dandc1@hoosierlink.com</A> .<P>Thanx.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||07-31-2001||11:55 PM||||i'm in the air guard. i think josh near me is in the army guard. i don't know exactly about communications, plus it seems like communications is the title of 3-4 different fields. i do guidance and control and we share a shop with communications and navigation, but there is also a comm shop that is a compleatly different field, and also a couple other fields where communications are part of what they do but i don't know anything about them. guess you have to talk to a recruiter.<P>we've had a few people transfer from the army and army guard/reserves to our base. i don't think they had to do basic again, but i think tech school would depend on what you were doing before and where you want to go in the air force.<P>guess i haven't told you anything definitive. talk to a recruiter.<P>small incentive...a friend joined the army guard when i joined the air. his first trip, a tent in the geogia woods. mine, a hotel in germany (with a day trip to paris thrown in). the last few years one of the trips for a/c maintenance has been a trip to porto rico. <p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||08-01-2001||02:28 AM||||MM, I leave October 4th for Basic.  It's in Texas, near San Antonio, specifically Lackland AFB.  It's only 6 weeks.  From what I have heard it can be hard but its not nearly as bad as Army or Marines.  I believe there are some other AF guys on this forum who could offer more insight.<P>I picked the AF because I am going to be a pilot.  It seems to be the best choice out of all the services, but thats just my opinion.<P>My advice would be to go and talk to a recruitor, my recruitor is awesome, he knows everything about the AF.  I wasn't too sure either but every question I had he knew the answer to right away.  If you want I could give you his number.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||08-01-2001||03:54 AM||||Speaking as an X-ARMY flier, I would rather go over the top of the problem instead of being in it! Air Force OR Navy would be my choices for today! As long as I was in a flying slot!<P>Too bad that wasn't the case 26 years ago!||||reg||1
Z||000004||AusFiero||08-01-2001||06:25 AM||||I know I wouldn't want to be in any of the Australian "Armed" Forces. They are armed with the stuff your guys sell off as obsolete  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Well ok they did try to build 2 new submarines locally. They leaked and the weapons wouldn't work. Thank god we make great Fiero parts  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||08-01-2001||12:03 PM||||black88gt, i did af basic about 16 yrs. ago and i've heard it's a little easier now, except that they've throw in some time out in the field. i don't know about that. after i did it i really thought it wasn't that hard. it's just not fun at all (i've pretty much decided since then that prison's probaly worse so imma be really good). just do what they tell you and don't try to impress them by doing more than you were told or try to get away with doing less. the goal for me was to try not to have it last more than 6 weeks. i came out an honor grad.<P>oh,and when hey give out barracks jobs try to avoid the latrine. especially try not to be the latrine queen   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||KissMySSFiero||08-01-2001||02:14 PM||||you say your 33.  you may want to look into the age limits.  i enlisted in the marines about a year ago with someone who was about 30.  he couldnt go because of his age.  he had to get all kinds of waivers and  release forms and write a letter to someone explaining why he thinks he should go.  he ended sayin just screw it.  too much hassle.  the reserves may be different though.  i want to go into the air guard or some type of reserves soon.  marines didnt pan out(screwed up my ankle 2 weeks prior to leaving....thank you lord)  i have a friend that is in the AF reserves and he loves it.  he got a $10k signing bonus and school is paid for.  plus ge makes about $200 a month from his one weekend.  not bad if you ask me.  <P>if you dont mind post anything you find out on here for me to check out.<BR>good luck<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||14
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||08-01-2001||02:22 PM||||When I was in the coast guard we had reserves come out every weekend to help on the rescue boats - the only advice I would give is if you will be doing something you like then give it a shot.<P> but if you are doing it for the money, you can make a LOT more than $200 a month if you invest 48 hours a month into a private business - easily! <P>  if you do it for the money after about the 3rd month your gonna hate it and be miserable.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||08-01-2001||02:27 PM||||Hey cool MM<BR>from what I know if your prior service you wont have to go to bootcamp for the Air Force  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||Monkeyman||08-01-2001||06:07 PM||||KissMySSFiero--The age limit is 36 (I called and checked today) so I'm still good to go for another couple, three years.  I'm looking for a large sign on bonus and paid schooling.<P>84Bill--Almost true.  If you've been to Basic Training in the last 6 months or so, I don't think you have to go again.  Unfortunately, I ETSed from the Army a little over 10 years ago.  I'll have to go to Basic.  But...It's only 6 weeks long (as opposed to 8 weeks for the Army) and from what I understand, it's geared more towards education than pure physical torture.  Still have to pass a PT test, I'm positive but it might be slightly more civilized than the Army Basic Training I went to back in 198something.<P>I'm still waiting on a call from a recruiter.  I don't plan on believing much that he says (unless it's in writing).  This is why I want someone (or lots of someones) to clue me in as to what it's really like these days.<P>I'll post here anything that I hear.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||08-01-2001||06:20 PM||||Heheh <BR>I did my basic at Ft. Dix and AIT in Ft Eustus from march to aug of 86, MAN was it hot in Va.||||reg||14
Z||000011||Black88GT||08-02-2001||12:34 AM||||MM, the PT test for the AF is something like 38 pushups in 2 min. and 2 miles in 18 min.  Not hard at all (well maybe for an old fogey like yourself hehe j/k).<P>My recruitor never BSed me once about anything.  He never promised me jack.  He basically laid it all out on the table for me one day and I made my own choices.  No hassling phone calls, no unexpected drop-bys (I live about 5 min. from his office btw) or any crap like that.  Now, the Marine recruitor was a little different.  Man that guy called my house non-stop like I owed a bill or something, even AFTER I signed with the AF!!  Geesh, I finally had to ask my recruitor to leave him a friendly note asking to leave me alone.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||08-02-2001||09:13 PM||||MM, you probably know we have a pretty large base here(WPAFB - Wright Patterson AFB), and there were several guys in my class that joined the AF after graduating.<P>They all told me that basic training was pretty easy. One of my friends(the brightest computer mind I've met) got stuck changing oil in Hummers for 6 months after basic training, but he was (verbally) told he would be dealing with electronics when he enlisted, he is slowly working his way into electronics now. All of them said get everything in writing when you join. Thats the suggestion that has been passed to me.<P>Jeff had it good it sounds like, but not all recruiters are alike. Pay and school funding are the reasons most people I know join.||||reg||1
Z||000013||maryjane||08-02-2001||09:48 PM||||Quote: I have no intention of becoming a Jarhead.(Nothing personal, Marines)<BR>That's ok, Monkeyman. No offense taken. But ask Scott O'Grady what he thinks of Jarheads.<BR>Good Luck and<BR>Semper Fi<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 2.5<BR>not slow not fast-just halfast||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tonker||08-02-2001||10:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B>I know I wouldn't want to be in any of the Australian "Armed" Forces. They are armed with the stuff your guys sell off as obsolete   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL Aus. We have it even worse than you guys, I think. We get the stuff that the Yanks sell off as obsolete and the Aussies aren't interested in.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>In fact I believe our Chief of Defence Staff had to be shuttled around in an Australian helicopter while visiting our ships on manoeuvres, because ours were either unsafe or grounded. Our Sea King helicopters have this nasty habit of falling into the drink, you see...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||2birds||08-03-2001||12:00 AM||||Monkeyman, <BR>From my days as a Navy recruiter back in the early 90's, I remember the Air Force recruiter was the "Chair Force" recruiter, because he didn't have to work too hard. People were beating a path to his door, while we were happy to get his leftovers.  1 USAF guy to 8 Army recruiters, 4 Marines, and 2 Navy. The USAF was a take it or leave it program.  I would imagine the reserve program is even more so.  Seems like most people assumed either they could fly, or it was easier, since they live in "dormitories" instead of barracks like the rest of us. <BR>So don't feel like he's blowing you off just yet. Each month has a new contract goal, and you try to keep yourself set up for months ahead of time. <P>Black88GT,<BR>In order to be a pilot, the general rule is you have to be a commissioned officer, and to be an officer takes a lot more than just having a Bachelor's degree (at least in my nuclear submarine world).  Work hard, set challenging goals, and don't procrastinate.  The USAF has the Community Colege of the Air Force, which is accredited.  Use it to your advantage, and best of luck to you. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Black88GT||08-03-2001||12:10 AM||||2birds, yeah, I was planning on getting a degree while I was in there.  Once I become a CO then I will apply to flight school.  <P>When I went to MEPS, they had a contract that had everything in writing, basically guaranteeing my job, so I won't be doing any oil changes lol.<P>School funding was one of the major reasons I joined also.  The AF just raised their tuition assistance from 75% to 100%, not to mention the G.I. Bill that all servicemen/women receive.  I figure if I can make it out of flight school, I can get out after 4 years and become a commercial airline pilot.||||reg||1
Z||000017||2birds||08-03-2001||06:17 PM||||Black88GT,<BR>Most of the officers on my sub decided to be nukes instead of aviators because the Navy required a 10 year commitment after flight training.  Hope the USAF isn't as tight.  <BR>Either way, that's great about the tuition assistance going up.  They just added another 6 grand to the GI Bill, for only a $600 investment.  I finished my Bachelor's without using it, so I can still use it for a Master's or a different degree after I retire.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Black88GT||08-05-2001||04:18 PM||||I have a meeting on Tuesday with my recruitor, I will ask him about the commitment.  I think its 6 years but I am not sure.  Even so, I would then probably stick it out a few more years to get a pension (another thing I have to ask my recruitor about).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||2birds||08-05-2001||04:39 PM||||20 years to retire with 50% of your base pay.  30 years and you can get 75%.  I'm about to pass 16.  Doesn't seem like I've been in this long, though.  I will have spent 11 years riding submarines, with roughly half of that time submerged.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
